tel 1.0

base iridescence
import soundness

info   colours in every model — sRGB, HSL, HSV, CIELAB, XYZ, CMY, CMYK — converted, mixed, adjusted and packed
detail
    _Iridescence_ represents a colour in whichever model suits the task — `Srgb` for a
    screen, `Cielab` for perceptual distance, `Hsl` or `Hsv` for intuitive adjustment, `Cmy`
    and `Cmyk` for print, `Xyz` as the device-independent hub — and converts between them
    with one method, `to[Model]`. Each model is its own type, so the compiler knows which a
    value is in, and a conversion which depends on viewing conditions cannot be written
    without a `Colorimetry` in scope to say what the illuminant is.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    given Colorimetry = colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

    val red: Srgb = Srgb(0.8, 0.2, 0.2)
    val lab: Cielab = red.to[Cielab]
    val slate: Hsl = Hsl(208.8.deg, 0.3, 0.4)
    slate.rotate(180.deg).lighten(0.2).to[Srgb]
    WebColors.DeepPink.to[Cielab].delta(WebColors.Tomato.to[Cielab])
    ```

    Around it: the 141 named `WebColors`; `rgb"#abcdef"`, a hexadecimal literal checked as
    the code compiles, which gives the whole-number `Chroma` a terminal expects and converts
    to a `Color in Srgb` with `color`; mixing, pairwise with `mix` or by parts with `Daub`
    arithmetic (`5*Red + 3*Yellow`) under a blend mode imported from `mixing`; `Theme` and
    `Palette` for coordinated sets of colours, with Solarized built in and chosen by
    `luminosity`; and `Pixel[layout]`, a colour packed into an integer in any channel layout,
    with `Rgb12` and `Rgb32` for two fixed packings.

    The library is one source set, `iridescence.core`, which depends on anticipation's
    `Chroma`, `Chromatic` and `Channel` types, hypotenuse and geodesy's `Angle`, and compiles
    for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native (`Palette#selectDynamic` uses reflection, which
    Native lacks; the named roles are ordinary methods). Everything is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella, and the `colorimetry`, `luminosity`, `mixing` and `themes` choice
    packages merge with the umbrella's own: their givens are imported by name, since
    `import soundness.*` does not import givens, and each selects a policy — an illuminant,
    a light or dark variant, a blend mode, a theme — which should never be implicit.

refer iridescence.Color
refer iridescence.Srgb
refer iridescence.Cielab
refer iridescence.Hsl
refer iridescence.Daub
refer iridescence.WebColors
refer iridescence.Pixel
refer iridescence.rgb

entry .Color   a colour in some model, converted to another with `to[Model]`
  detail
      The trait every colour model extends. A `Color`'s type member `Form` is its own model,
      and `to[Model]` converts it through the `Perceptual` instance from `Form` to `Model`,
      found in the companions of the two models. The prepositional spelling `Color in Srgb`
      names a colour whose `Form` is `Srgb`, and is the type `WebColors` entries and `Palette`
      roles have; a concrete `Srgb` is one.

      The instances declared are `Srgb` to and from each of `Xyz`,
      `Cielab`, `Cmy`, `Cmyk`, `Hsl` and `Hsv`, `Xyz` to and from `Cielab`, and `Cmy` to and
      from `Cmyk`. Every model reaches every other through `Srgb`, but the caller writes
      the hop: `hsl.to[Xyz]` has no instance, where `hsl.to[Srgb].to[Xyz]` does.
      The hops between `Srgb` and `Xyz` or `Cielab` need a `Colorimetry` in scope, since
      those models are defined relative to a reference white.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A colour in sRGB
        code
            import soundness.*
            import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

            val tomato: Srgb = WebColors.Tomato.to[Srgb]   // Srgb(1, 0.388, 0.278)
      frame
        label Into HSL, where hue is an angle
        code
            import soundness.*
            import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

            val hsl: Hsl = WebColors.Tomato.to[Hsl]        // Hsl(9.1°, 1, 0.639)
      frame
        label Into XYZ, relative to the daylight reference white
        code
            import soundness.*
            import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

            val xyz: Xyz = WebColors.Tomato.to[Hsl].to[Srgb].to[Xyz]   // back through Srgb, needing the Colorimetry
      frame
        label Into CIELAB, where distance is perceptual
        code
            import soundness.*
            import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

            val lab: Cielab = WebColors.Tomato.to[Hsl].to[Srgb].to[Xyz].to[Cielab]
            lab.delta(WebColors.OrangeRed.to[Cielab])      // small: the two look alike
      frame
        label And back to sRGB, within rounding
        code
            import soundness.*
            import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

            WebColors.Tomato.to[Hsl].to[Srgb].to[Xyz].to[Cielab].to[Srgb]   // Srgb(1, 0.388, 0.278), near enough
      ##
      ```

      A colour is `Pure`: it holds no capabilities, so it may be stored and shared freely.
      Every colour is `Chromatic` (`.chromatic`), so it is accepted wherever a terminal or an
      image wants a `Chroma`, and `n*color` lifts it to a `Daub` of `n` parts for mixing.
  refer iridescence.Perceptual
  refer iridescence.Colorimetry
  refer iridescence.Daub
  entry .chromatic   any colour with a conversion to `Srgb` is `Chromatic`: a 24-bit `Chroma` for terminals and images
      Converts to `Srgb` and scales each channel to `0`–`255`, truncating (`0.5` is `127`).
      This is what lets `e"${WebColors.Red}(text)"` colour terminal text, and
      `Fg(WebColors.Red)` take a colour directly. `Pixel#chroma` rounds instead, so a colour
      packed and unpacked may differ from this by one in a channel.
  entry .multiplicable   `parts*color` for a `Double`: a `Daub` of that many parts of the colour
      The `Multiplicable` instance lives here rather than in `Daub`'s companion because
      `Daub` appears only in the result: the implicit scope of `0.5*WebColors.Red` is built
      from `Double` and the colour's type. A `Color in Srgb` and a plain `Srgb` both lift to
      the same `Daub[Srgb]`, so the two can be added to one another.
  entry .wholeMultiplicable   `parts*color` for an `Int`: a `Daub` of that many parts of the colour
      `5*WebColors.Red` is five parts red. The parts matter only when daubs are added.
  entry #Form   the colour's own model: `Srgb` for an `Srgb`, and so on
  entry #to   the colour converted to another model: `color.to[Cielab]`
      Needs a `Form is Perceptual in Model` instance, which the companions supply for every
      pair, and for `Srgb`, `Xyz` or `Cielab` as source or target (other than
      `Xyz` to `Srgb`) a `Colorimetry` in scope. Converting a colour to its own model is the
      identity. The result is the target's concrete type, so `to[Hsl].rotate(…)` chains.

entry .Srgb   a colour as red, green and blue fractions of full intensity, the screen's model
  detail
      `Srgb(red, green, blue)`, each in `0`–`1`. The model everything else converts through:
      the `Chromatic` instance renders a colour by converting here, `Palette` and `Spectrum`
      compare colours here, and `WebColors` are defined here. Standard sRGB gamma is applied
      on the way to `Xyz`, with the D65 primaries.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val mauve: Srgb = Srgb(0.878, 0.690, 1.0)
      mauve.mix(WebColors.White.to[Srgb], 0.25)   // a quarter of the way to white
      mauve.to[Hsl].hue.degrees                   // 276.4
      rgb"#e0b0ff".color                          // the same colour, as Color in Srgb
      ```
  refer iridescence.Color
  refer iridescence.WebColors
  entry .xyz   `Srgb` to `Xyz`: linearises each channel and applies the sRGB matrix; needs a `Colorimetry`
  entry .cielab   `Srgb` to `Cielab`, through `Xyz`; needs a `Colorimetry`
  entry .cmy   `Srgb` to `Cmy`: each channel's complement
  entry .cmyk   `Srgb` to `Cmyk`, through `Cmy`
  entry .hsl   `Srgb` to `Hsl`: hue as an `Angle` on the colour circle, saturation and lightness
      A grey (all channels equal) has hue `0` and saturation `0`.
  entry .hsv   `Srgb` to `Hsv`: hue as an `Angle`, saturation and value (the brightest channel)
  entry #red   the red channel, `0`–`1`
  entry #green   the green channel, `0`–`1`
  entry #blue   the blue channel, `0`–`1`
  entry #mix   this colour and another, blended channel by channel in a ratio
      `mix(that, ratio)` is `(1 - ratio)` of this and `ratio` of `that` in each channel, so
      `mix(that)` (ratio `0.5`) is the average, `mix(that, 0)` this colour and `mix(that, 1)`
      the other. A blend of screen values; `Cielab#mix` blends what the eye perceives.

entry .Hsl   a colour as hue, saturation and lightness: the model for adjusting a colour
  detail
      `Hsl(hue, saturation, lightness)`, with the hue an `Angle` — a point on the colour
      circle, so the unit is written (`208.8.deg`, `π.rad`) rather than assumed — and the
      other two in `0`–`1`. Its methods are the adjustments a person reaches for: `rotate`
      the hue, `saturate` or `desaturate`, `lighten` or `darken`, take the `complement`.
      Convert into `Hsl`, adjust, convert back.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val tomato: Hsl = WebColors.Tomato.to[Hsl]
      tomato.rotate(180.deg).to[Srgb]          // the complementary colour
      tomato.lighten(0.2).to[Srgb]             // a fifth of the way toward white
      tomato.desaturate.to[Srgb]               // the same lightness, no colour
      tomato.rotate((-30).deg).hue.degrees     // 339.1: the hue wraps, never negative
      ```

      `Hsl` has no `Blendable` instance, so daubs of it cannot be added: averaging two hues
      coordinatewise takes the long way round the circle whenever the pair straddles zero,
      and two reds would mix to cyan. Mix in `Srgb` or `Cielab` instead.
  refer iridescence.Hsv
  refer iridescence.Srgb
  entry .perceptual   `Hsl` to `Srgb`
  entry #hue   the hue, as an `Angle` around the colour circle
  entry #saturation   the saturation, `0` (grey) to `1` (pure)
  entry #lightness   the lightness, `0` (black) to `1` (white)
  entry #saturate   the same hue and lightness at full saturation
  entry #desaturate   the same lightness with no saturation: a grey
  entry #rotate   the hue turned by an angle, wrapped to its principal value
      `rotate(360.deg)` leaves the hue alone, and a backward turn past zero wraps to the top
      of the circle; a turn landing exactly on zero is `0`, not a full turn.
  entry #complement   the hue turned half a circle: `rotate(180.deg)`
  entry #pure   the hue at full saturation and middle lightness: the purest colour of that hue
  entry #lighten   lightness moved a fraction of the way toward `1`
      `lighten(0.5)` on a lightness of `0.4` gives `0.7`; `lighten(1)` is white.
  entry #darken   lightness moved a fraction of the way toward `0`
      `darken(0.5)` on a lightness of `0.4` gives `0.2`; `darken(1)` is black.

entry .Hsv   a colour as hue, saturation and value, with tints, shades and tones
  detail
      `Hsv(hue, saturation, value)`: the hue an `Angle`, saturation in `0`–`1`, and value the
      intensity of the brightest channel in `0`–`1`. It adjusts as `Hsl` does — `rotate`,
      `saturate`, `desaturate`, `complement`, `pure` — and adds the painter's vocabulary:
      `tint` mixes toward white, `shade` toward black and `tone` toward both.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val sky: Hsv = Hsv(200.deg, 0.8, 0.9)
      sky.tint(0.3)      // less saturated: toward white
      sky.shade(0.3)     // less value: toward black
      sky.tone(0.2, 0.2) // both
      ```

      Like `Hsl`, `Hsv` has no `Blendable` instance, since hue is circular.
  refer iridescence.Hsl
  entry .perceptual   `Hsv` to `Srgb`
  entry #hue   the hue, as an `Angle`
  entry #saturation   the saturation, `0`–`1`
  entry #value   the value: the intensity of the strongest channel, `0`–`1`
  entry #saturate   the same hue and value at full saturation
  entry #desaturate   the same value with no saturation
  entry #rotate   the hue turned by an angle, wrapped to its principal value
  entry #complement   the hue turned half a circle
  entry #pure   the hue at full saturation and full value
  entry #shade   the colour darkened: the value scaled by `1 - black`
      `shade(0)` is the colour unchanged; `shade(1)` has value `0`.
  entry #tint   the colour whitened: the saturation scaled by `1 - white`
      `tint(0)` is the colour unchanged; `tint(1)` has saturation `0`.
  entry #tone   `shade(black)` then `tint(white)`

entry .Xyz   CIE 1931 XYZ: the device-independent model every perceptual conversion passes through
  detail
      `Xyz(x, y, z)`, the tristimulus values on the scale where the reference white has
      `y = 100`. Nothing is adjusted here; it is the hub between `Srgb` and `Cielab`.
      Converting to `Cielab` divides by the reference white of the `Colorimetry` in scope;
      converting to `Srgb` applies the inverse sRGB matrix and gamma, and needs no
      `Colorimetry`.
  refer iridescence.Cielab
  refer iridescence.Colorimetry
  entry .cielab   `Xyz` to `Cielab`, relative to the `Colorimetry`'s 2° reference white
  entry .srgb   `Xyz` to `Srgb`: the inverse sRGB matrix, then gamma
  entry #x   the X tristimulus value
  entry #y   the Y tristimulus value: luminance, `100` for the reference white
  entry #z   the Z tristimulus value

entry .Cielab   CIE L*a*b*: the model where distance between colours matches how different they look
  detail
      `Cielab(lightness, blueYellow, greenRed)`: L* from `0` (black) to `100` (white), and the
      two opponent axes, a* (`greenRed`, negative toward green) and b* (`blueYellow`, negative
      toward blue), each roughly `-128`–`127`. The space is built so that equal distances
      correspond to equal perceived differences, which `#delta` measures: a small delta
      means two colours are hard to tell apart. It is the right model for choosing a readable
      foreground against a background, where raw RGB distances mislead, and `#mix` here
      gives a more natural midpoint than mixing screen values.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

      val pink: Cielab = WebColors.DeepPink.to[Cielab]
      pink.delta(WebColors.Tomato.to[Cielab])
      WebColors.Ivory.to[Cielab].mix(pink)   // the perceptual midpoint
      Cielab(0, 0, 0).delta(Cielab(3, 4, 12))   // 13.0: a Euclidean distance
      ```

      Its coordinates are not fractions of `0`–`1`, so it is not `Tonal`: daubs of it add
      under `proportionalMixing` only, and `import mixing.multiplyMixing` with CIELAB does not
      compile.
  refer iridescence.Xyz
  refer iridescence.Colorimetry
  entry .xyz   `Cielab` to `Xyz`, relative to the `Colorimetry`'s 2° reference white
  entry .srgb   `Cielab` to `Srgb`, through `Xyz`; needs a `Colorimetry`
  entry #lightness   L*: `0` black to `100` white
  entry #blueYellow   b*: negative toward blue, positive toward yellow
  entry #greenRed   a*: negative toward green, positive toward red
  entry #delta   the Euclidean distance to another colour on all three axes: ΔE*ab
  entry #mix   this colour and another, blended on all three axes in a ratio
      `mix(that)` is the midpoint; `mix(that, ratio)` is `ratio` of the way toward `that`.

entry .Cmy   a colour as cyan, magenta and yellow: the subtractive complement of sRGB
  detail
      `Cmy(cyan, magenta, yellow)`, each in `0`–`1`, and each the complement of the
      corresponding sRGB channel: `Srgb(1 - c, 1 - m, 1 - y)`. It is the step between `Srgb`
      and `Cmyk`, and a `Tonal` space, so it mixes under any blend mode.
  refer iridescence.Cmyk
  entry .cmyk   `Cmy` to `Cmyk`: the common part of the three inks becomes the key (black)
      The key is the least of the three (or `1`, giving pure black); the inks are what remains
      after it, rescaled.
  entry .srgb   `Cmy` to `Srgb`: each channel's complement
  entry #cyan   the cyan ink, `0`–`1`
  entry #magenta   the magenta ink, `0`–`1`
  entry #yellow   the yellow ink, `0`–`1`

entry .Cmyk   a colour as cyan, magenta, yellow and key (black): the printer's model
  detail
      `Cmyk(cyan, magenta, yellow, key)`, each in `0`–`1`. The key is the black ink which
      replaces equal parts of the other three; `Cmyk(0, 0, 0, 1)` is black. A `Tonal` space,
      so it mixes under any blend mode. `Cmyk8` is the 32-bit packed layout of the same
      channels; this type is the `Double`-based model.
  refer iridescence.Cmy
  entry .cmy   `Cmyk` to `Cmy`: the key added back into each ink
  entry .srgb   `Cmyk` to `Srgb`, through `Cmy`
  entry #cyan   the cyan ink, `0`–`1`
  entry #magenta   the magenta ink, `0`–`1`
  entry #yellow   the yellow ink, `0`–`1`
  entry #key   the black ink, `0`–`1`

entry .Perceptual   the typeclass of conversions between colour models: `Form is Perceptual in Model`
  detail
      `color.to[Model]` summons a `color.Form is Perceptual in Model` and calls its
      `#convert`. Instances are declared in the companions of the models they convert from
      (`Srgb.hsl`, `Cielab.xyz`, …), so they resolve with no import; a new model joins the
      library by extending `Color` with `type Form = NewModel` and declaring a `Perceptual`
      to and from `Srgb` in its companion. The companion's `identity` instance makes a
      conversion to a colour's own model free, and `Daub.perceptual` lets a daub convert as
      the colour it holds.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Grey(level: Double) extends Color:
        type Form = Grey

      object Grey:
        given srgb: Grey is Perceptual in Srgb = grey => Srgb(grey.level, grey.level, grey.level)

      Grey(0.5).to[Srgb]
      ```
  refer iridescence.Color
  entry .identity   any colour converts to its own model unchanged
  entry #Self   the model converted from
  entry #Form   the model converted to
  entry #convert   the conversion, from a colour of `Self` to one of `Form`

entry .Blendable   the typeclass of models whose colours combine coordinate by coordinate
  detail
      Blending works one coordinate at a time, so a model needs only to say how a pointwise
      operation over its coordinates rebuilds a colour: `#zip(left, right, operation)`.
      `Daub` addition needs it, together with a `Mixing` mode. `Srgb`, `Cmy`, `Cmyk`,
      `Cielab` and `Xyz` have instances in the companion; `Hsl` and `Hsv` deliberately have
      none, since their hue is an angle on a circle and averaging two hues coordinatewise
      would take the long way round whenever the pair straddles zero — two reds would give
      cyan.
  refer iridescence.Daub
  refer iridescence.Mixing
  entry .srgb   `Srgb` blends on red, green and blue
  entry .cmy   `Cmy` blends on cyan, magenta and yellow
  entry .cmyk   `Cmyk` blends on cyan, magenta, yellow and key
  entry .cielab   `Cielab` blends on L*, a* and b*
  entry .xyz   `Xyz` blends on X, Y and Z
  entry #Self   the model whose colours blend
  entry #zip   two colours combined by a function applied to each pair of coordinates

entry .Tonal   marks a model whose coordinates all run from `0` to `1`, so every blend mode applies to it
  detail
      `Srgb`, `Cmy` and `Cmyk` are `Tonal`. Every `Mixing` mode but `proportionalMixing`
      reads coordinates as fractions of full intensity — `multiply` is a product of
      coordinates, `screen` a product of their complements — and neither describes anything
      where a coordinate can be `100` or negative, so the modes are offered only for `Tonal`
      models: `import mixing.multiplyMixing` and `1*Cielab(0, 0, 0) + 1*Cielab(40, 20, 10)` is
      a compile error rather than a silent proportional mix. `Cielab` and `Xyz` have no
      instance.
  refer iridescence.Mixing
  entry .srgb   `Srgb` is `Tonal`
  entry .cmy   `Cmy` is `Tonal`
  entry .cmyk   `Cmyk` is `Tonal`
  entry #Self   the model whose coordinates are all fractions

entry .Mixing   the typeclass of blend modes: how a layer's coordinate combines with the backdrop under it
  detail
      `#blend(backdrop, layer)` is one of the separable blend modes of Photoshop and GIMP, in
      the form the W3C compositing specification gives them. When daubs are added, the mode
      is applied at full strength and the result mixed back in the added daub's share of the
      total parts — exactly what a layer's opacity slider does — so only `proportionalMixing`
      is commutative; under every other mode `5*Red + 3*Yellow` differs from
      `3*Yellow + 5*Red`, as reordering two layers does.

      There is deliberately no default instance in this companion: a mode reachable through
      the implicit scope would be found whenever the imported one did not apply, so mixing
      CIELAB under `import mixing.multiplyMixing` would quietly fall back to a proportional
      mix rather than being rejected. The mode is always named by an import from `mixing`.
  refer iridescence.mixing
  refer iridescence.Daub
  entry #Self   the model this blend mode applies to
  entry #blend   the layer's coordinate laid over the backdrop's, under this mode

entry .Daub   an amount of paint: a colour with the number of parts of it going into a mixture
  detail
      `5*WebColors.Red` is a `Daub[Srgb]` of five parts red (through `Color.wholeMultiplicable`,
      or `Color.multiplicable` for a `Double`). A daub is itself a `Color` — five parts red is
      still red — so an expression such as `5*Red + 3*Yellow` converts, renders and measures
      like any colour, and `#parts` matters only when another daub is added to it. Adding
      needs the model to be `Blendable` and a `Mixing` mode imported from `mixing`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Five parts red
        code
            import soundness.*
            import mixing.proportionalMixing

            val paint = 5*WebColors.Red
            paint.to[Srgb]                     // Srgb(1, 0, 0): still red
      frame
        label Add three parts yellow
        code
            import soundness.*
            import mixing.proportionalMixing

            val paint = 5*WebColors.Red + 3*WebColors.Yellow
            paint.to[Srgb]                     // Srgb(1, 0.375, 0): an orange
            paint.to[Hsl].hue.degrees          // 22.5
      frame
        label Add two parts white: weighted against the eight parts so far
        code
            import soundness.*
            import mixing.proportionalMixing

            val paint = 5*WebColors.Red + 3*WebColors.Yellow + 2*WebColors.White
            paint.to[Srgb]                     // Srgb(1, 0.5, 0.2)
      frame
        label The same daubs under a different mode
        code
            import soundness.*
            import mixing.multiplyMixing

            val paint = 5*WebColors.Red + 3*WebColors.Yellow + 2*WebColors.White
            paint.to[Srgb]                     // darker: each layer multiplies the one beneath
      ##
      ```

      Parts are proportions, so `5*Red + 3*Yellow` and `50*Red + 30*Yellow` are the same
      colour, and a third daub is weighted against the total so far rather than against its
      neighbour: `1*Red + 1*Lime + 1*Blue` is an even third of each. A daub of zero parts
      takes no part at all — `0*Red + 1*Blue` under `multiply` is blue, not darkened by red.
      A `Color in Srgb` and a plain `Srgb` lift to the same `Daub[Srgb]`, so `WebColors`
      entries and literals mix together.
  refer iridescence.Mixing
  refer iridescence.mixing
  refer iridescence.Blendable
  entry .addable   `left + right`: the right daub laid over the left in its share of the parts, under the `Mixing` in scope
      Needs `topic is Blendable` and `topic is Mixing`. The result has the sum of the parts
      and, coordinate by coordinate, `backdrop + (mode(backdrop, layer) - backdrop)*share`
      where `share` is the right daub's fraction of the new total. A daub of no parts on
      either side returns the other unchanged.
  entry .perceptual   a daub converts to any model its colour converts to
  entry #parts   how many parts of the colour: the weight it carries when another daub is added
  entry #color   the colour itself, in its model

entry .Colorimetry   the reference white a perceptual conversion assumes: an illuminant and observer
  detail
      `Colorimetry(x2, y2, z2, x10, y10, z10)` holds the XYZ tristimulus values of the
      reference white for the CIE 1931 2° observer and the CIE 1964 10° observer. Conversions
      between `Xyz` and `Cielab` (and so between `Srgb` and `Cielab`) divide by it, so the
      same coordinates mean a different colour under daylight and under a tungsten bulb;
      the conversions use the 2° values. One is chosen by importing a given from the
      `colorimetry` package, or bound locally; there is no default, since presuming the
      illuminant would be presuming the viewing conditions.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Colorimetry = colorimetry.incandescentTungstenColorimetry

      WebColors.Ivory.to[Cielab]   // computed for tungsten light
      ```
  refer iridescence.colorimetry
  refer iridescence.Cielab
  entry #x2   X of the reference white, 2° observer
  entry #y2   Y of the reference white, 2° observer: `100`
  entry #z2   Z of the reference white, 2° observer
  entry #x10   X of the reference white, 10° observer
  entry #y10   Y of the reference white, 10° observer: `100`
  entry #z10   Z of the reference white, 10° observer

entry .colorimetry   the standard illuminants, each a `Colorimetry` given to import by name
  detail
      The CIE standard illuminants and the common fluorescent lamps, by name:
      `daylightColorimetry` is D65, the illuminant sRGB and most screen work assume, and
      suits almost every program. The others model incandescent light (A), direct sunlight
      (B), old daylight (C), the printing profile connection space (D50), mid-morning
      daylight (D55), north-sky daylight (D75), the equal-energy illuminant (E), and the
      fluorescent series F1 to F12. Several are aliases of another: `srgbColorimetry` and
      `adobeRgbColorimetry` are D65; `d65SimulatorColorimetry` is F7; `d50SimulatorColorimetry`
      is F8 (`sylvaniaF40Colorimetry`); `ultralume50Colorimetry`, `ultralume40Colorimetry` and
      `ultralume30Colorimetry` are F10, F11 and F12.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

      WebColors.Ivory.to[Cielab]
      ```
  entry .incandescentTungstenColorimetry   illuminant A: incandescent tungsten, 2856 K
  entry .oldDirectSunlightAtNoonColorimetry   illuminant B: direct sunlight at noon, obsolete
  entry .oldDaylightColorimetry   illuminant C: average north-sky daylight, obsolete
  entry .iccProfilePcsColorimetry   illuminant D50: horizon light, the ICC profile connection space
  entry .midMorningDaylightColorimetry   illuminant D55: mid-morning or mid-afternoon daylight
  entry .daylightColorimetry   illuminant D65: noon daylight, the sRGB reference white; the usual choice
  entry .srgbColorimetry   the sRGB reference white: D65, the same as `daylightColorimetry`
  entry .adobeRgbColorimetry   the Adobe RGB reference white: D65, the same as `daylightColorimetry`
  entry .northSkyDaylightColorimetry   illuminant D75: north-sky daylight
  entry .equalEnergyColorimetry   illuminant E: equal energy at every wavelength
  entry .daylightFluorescentF1Colorimetry   illuminant F1: daylight fluorescent, 6430 K
  entry .coolFluorescentColorimetry   illuminant F2: cool white fluorescent, 4230 K
  entry .whiteFluorescentColorimetry   illuminant F3: white fluorescent, 3450 K
  entry .warmWhiteFluorescentColorimetry   illuminant F4: warm white fluorescent, 2940 K
  entry .daylightFluorescentF5Colorimetry   illuminant F5: daylight fluorescent, 6350 K
  entry .liteWhiteFluorescentColorimetry   illuminant F6: light white fluorescent, 4150 K
  entry .daylightFluorescentF7Colorimetry   illuminant F7: a D65 simulator, 6500 K
  entry .d65SimulatorColorimetry   the D65 simulator: the same as `daylightFluorescentF7Colorimetry`
  entry .sylvaniaF40Colorimetry   illuminant F8: Sylvania F40, a D50 simulator, 5000 K
  entry .d50SimulatorColorimetry   the D50 simulator: the same as `sylvaniaF40Colorimetry`
  entry .coolWhiteFluorescentColorimetry   illuminant F9: cool white deluxe fluorescent, 4150 K
  entry .philipsTl85Colorimetry   illuminant F10: Philips TL85, 5000 K
  entry .ultralume50Colorimetry   Ultralume 50: the same as `philipsTl85Colorimetry`
  entry .philipsTl84Colorimetry   illuminant F11: Philips TL84, 4000 K
  entry .ultralume40Colorimetry   Ultralume 40: the same as `philipsTl84Colorimetry`
  entry .philipsTl83Colorimetry   illuminant F12: Philips TL83, 3000 K
  entry .ultralume30Colorimetry   Ultralume 30: the same as `philipsTl83Colorimetry`

entry .mixing   the blend modes, each a `Mixing` given to import by name before daubs are added
  detail
      `proportionalMixing` averages the colours by their parts, which is what pairwise
      `mix` does and the only mode which means anything in a model whose coordinates are not
      confined to `0`–`1`. The rest are the blend modes of Photoshop and GIMP, defined as the
      W3C compositing specification defines them, and are offered only for `Tonal` models
      (`Srgb`, `Cmy`, `Cmyk`): asking for one in `Cielab` does not compile. Exactly one is
      imported where daubs are added; none is in scope by default.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import mixing.multiplyMixing

      (1*WebColors.Yellow + 1*WebColors.Cyan).to[Srgb]   // Srgb(0.5, 1, 0): a green, as paint mixes
      ```

      A daub's share of the total parts acts as its opacity: the mode is applied at full
      strength and the result mixed back in that proportion, so `3*White + 1*Black` under
      `multiply` is `Srgb(0.75, 0.75, 0.75)` and `9*White + 1*Black` is `0.9` grey.
  refer iridescence.Daub
  refer iridescence.Mixing
  refer iridescence.Tonal
  entry .proportionalMixing   the layer replaces the backdrop: a weighted average by parts; any model
  entry .multiplyMixing   `backdrop × layer`: always darker, as inks or filters combine
  entry .screenMixing   `1 - (1 - backdrop)(1 - layer)`: always lighter, as projected lights combine
  entry .darkenMixing   the lesser coordinate of the two
  entry .lightenMixing   the greater coordinate of the two
  entry .differenceMixing   the absolute difference of the two
  entry .exclusionMixing   `backdrop + layer - 2·backdrop·layer`: like difference, with less contrast
  entry .linearDodgeMixing   the sum, capped at `1`
  entry .linearBurnMixing   the sum less `1`, floored at `0`
  entry .hardLightMixing   multiply where the layer is dark, screen where it is light
  entry .overlayMixing   multiply where the backdrop is dark, screen where it is light: hard light with the operands exchanged
  entry .softLightMixing   a gentler hard light, by the W3C formula
  entry .colorDodgeMixing   the backdrop brightened by the layer: `backdrop / (1 - layer)`, capped at `1`
  entry .colorBurnMixing   the backdrop darkened by the layer: `1 - (1 - backdrop) / layer`, floored at `0`

entry .Brightness   whether a theme is dark or light: the enum of `Dark` and `Light`
    The variant of a `Theme` which offers both, and the value `dark` and `light` test. One
    is chosen by importing `luminosity.darkBrightness` or `luminosity.lightBrightness`, so
    terminal output styled through a theme adapts to the terminal's background.

entry .luminosity   dark or light, each a `Brightness` given to import by name
  detail
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import luminosity.darkBrightness
      import themes.solarizedTheme

      summon[Theme].background   // Srgb(0, 0.169, 0.212): Solarized's dark base
      ```
  entry .darkBrightness   `Brightness.Dark`: the dark variant of a theme
  entry .lightBrightness   `Brightness.Light`: the light variant of a theme

entry .dark   whether the `Brightness` in scope is `Dark`
    An inline test of the contextual `Brightness`, for a theme or a palette choosing between
    its variants: `if dark then base03 else base3`.

entry .light   whether the `Brightness` in scope is `Light`

entry .Theme   a coordinated set of colours: a background, a foreground, accents and whether it is dark or light
  detail
      A `Theme` has a model (`#Form`, convertible to `Srgb`), a `#background` and
      `#foreground` in it, a list of accent `#colors`, and its `#luminosity`. Its
      `#spectrum` assigns the accents to the eight primary roles — red, yellow, green, blue,
      cyan, magenta, black, white — by nearest colour, so a program which wants "the theme's
      red" asks the spectrum. `Solarized` is the built-in theme, and `themes.solarizedTheme`
      the given which selects it in the `Brightness` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import luminosity.lightBrightness
      import themes.solarizedTheme

      val theme: Theme = summon[Theme]
      theme.luminosity          // Brightness.Light
      theme.foreground          // Srgb(0, 0.169, 0.212)
      theme.spectrum.red        // the accent nearest pure red
      ```
  refer iridescence.Solarized
  refer iridescence.Spectrum
  refer iridescence.Palette
  entry #Form   the model the theme's colours are in
  entry #luminosity   whether the theme is dark or light
  entry #background   the background colour
  entry #foreground   the foreground colour
  entry #colors   the theme's accent colours, from which the spectrum is assigned
  entry #spectrum   the accents assigned to the eight primary roles by nearest colour, once
      A `lazy val`, computed on first use from `#colors` with `Spectrum.apply`.

entry .Solarized   the Solarized theme's sixteen colours, as a `Theme` in `Srgb`
  detail
      Ethan Schoonover's palette: eight accents (`red`, `yellow`, `blue`, `green`, `orange`,
      `cyan`, `magenta`, `violet`) and eight monotones from `base03`, the darkest, to `base3`,
      the lightest. The trait leaves `luminosity`, `background` and `foreground` abstract;
      `themes.solarizedTheme` completes it from the `Brightness` in scope, with `base03` on
      `base3` for the dark variant and the reverse for the light one.
  refer iridescence.themes
  entry #red   `Srgb(0.863, 0.196, 0.184)`
  entry #yellow   `Srgb(0.710, 0.537, 0)`
  entry #blue   `Srgb(0.149, 0.545, 0.824)`
  entry #green   `Srgb(0.522, 0.600, 0)`
  entry #orange   `Srgb(0.796, 0.294, 0.086)`
  entry #cyan   `Srgb(0.165, 0.631, 0.596)`
  entry #magenta   `Srgb(0.827, 0.212, 0.510)`
  entry #violet   `Srgb(0.424, 0.443, 0.769)`
  entry #base03   the darkest monotone, `Srgb(0, 0.169, 0.212)`: the dark variant's background
  entry #base02   `Srgb(0.027, 0.212, 0.259)`
  entry #base01   `Srgb(0.345, 0.431, 0.459)`
  entry #base00   `Srgb(0.396, 0.482, 0.514)`
  entry #base0   `Srgb(0.514, 0.580, 0.588)`
  entry #base1   `Srgb(0.576, 0.631, 0.631)`
  entry #base2   `Srgb(0.933, 0.910, 0.835)`
  entry #base3   the lightest monotone, `Srgb(0.992, 0.965, 0.890)`: the light variant's background
  entry #colors   the eight accents, then `base3` and `base03`

entry .themes   the built-in themes, each a `Theme` given to import by name
  detail
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import luminosity.darkBrightness
      import themes.solarizedTheme

      summon[Theme].background   // Srgb(0, 0.169, 0.212)
      ```
  entry .solarizedTheme   Solarized, in the dark or light variant the `Brightness` in scope selects
      Needs a `Brightness` given — `luminosity.darkBrightness` or `luminosity.lightBrightness`
      — and is a `Solarized`, so its sixteen colours are reachable by name.

entry .Spectrum   a theme's accents assigned to the eight primary colour roles
  detail
      `Spectrum(colors)` takes a list of colours in any model convertible to `Srgb` and
      assigns one to each of `black`, `white`, `red`, `green`, `blue`, `yellow`, `cyan` and
      `magenta`, in that order, each the colour nearest (by sRGB distance) to the pure
      primary among those not yet assigned; when the list runs out it is refilled, so a
      short list serves every role. `Theme#spectrum` is one, computed from the theme's
      accents.
  refer iridescence.Theme
  entry .apply   `Spectrum(colors)`: the roles assigned by nearest colour, without repetition until the list is exhausted
      Panics only if the list is empty.
  entry #Form   the model the colours are in
  entry #red   the colour nearest pure red
  entry #yellow   the colour nearest pure yellow
  entry #green   the colour nearest pure green
  entry #blue   the colour nearest pure blue
  entry #cyan   the colour nearest pure cyan
  entry #magenta   the colour nearest pure magenta
  entry #black   the colour nearest black
  entry #white   the colour nearest white

entry .Palette   a set of colours by role, with `subdue` and `accent` to push one toward the background or away from it
  detail
      A `Palette` has a model (`#Form`), a `#background` and a `#foreground`, and whatever
      roles the trait extending it declares (`palettes.Reporting` adds `success`, `error`,
      `warning`; a library's own palette trait adds its own). `#subdue(color)` mixes a colour
      halfway toward the background and `#accent(color)` toward the foreground, and `#mix`
      blends any two roles, so a palette of a few roles yields many. The trait is `into`, so
      a value is adapted to it where a `Palette` is expected, and `Selectable`, so a role may
      be read by name with `selectDynamic` on the JVM.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Report extends Palette with palettes.Reporting:
        type Form = Srgb
        val background = rgb"#1e1e2e".color
        val foreground = rgb"#cdd6f4".color
        val success = rgb"#a6e3a1".color
        val error = rgb"#f38ba8".color
        val warning = rgb"#f9e2af".color

      Report.subdue(Report.error)        // the error colour, half faded into the background
      Report.accent(Report.success, 0.2) // the success colour, a fifth of the way to the foreground
      ```

      A palette written from hex literals gives each role a `rgb"…".color`, since the roles
      are `Color in Srgb`.
  refer iridescence.palettes
  refer iridescence.Theme
  entry #Form   the model the roles are in, convertible to `Srgb`
  entry #background   the background colour
  entry #foreground   the foreground colour
  entry #black   pure black, `Srgb(0, 0, 0)`
  entry #white   pure white, `Srgb(1, 1, 1)`
  entry #subdue   a colour mixed toward the background, halfway by default
  entry #accent   a colour mixed toward the foreground, halfway by default
  entry #mix   two colours blended channel by channel in sRGB, halfway by default
  entry #selectDynamic   a role read by its name, through reflection: `palette.selectDynamic("error")`
      JVM only: it calls the method of that name reflectively, so it is unavailable on
      Scala Native, where the named roles remain ordinary calls.

entry .palettes   role sets a `Palette` may declare: reporting outcomes, the terminal's sixteen colours, syntax
  detail
      Mix-in traits, each a self-type of `Palette`, which name the roles a kind of palette
      must supply, so that code styling a report, a terminal or source code can ask for a
      `Palette with palettes.Reporting` and address its roles by name.
  entry .Reporting   the roles of a palette for outcomes: `success`, `error` and `warning`
    detail
        A self-typed mix-in for `Palette`.
    entry #success   the colour of a success
    entry #error   the colour of an error
    entry #warning   the colour of a warning
  entry .Terminal   the sixteen ANSI colours as roles: `black` to `white` and `brightBlack` to `brightWhite`
    detail
        A self-typed mix-in for `Palette`, for a palette which reproduces or replaces the
        terminal's own sixteen-colour palette.
    entry #black   ANSI colour 0
    entry #red   ANSI colour 1
    entry #green   ANSI colour 2
    entry #yellow   ANSI colour 3
    entry #blue   ANSI colour 4
    entry #magenta   ANSI colour 5
    entry #cyan   ANSI colour 6
    entry #white   ANSI colour 7
    entry #brightBlack   ANSI colour 8
    entry #brightRed   ANSI colour 9
    entry #brightGreen   ANSI colour 10
    entry #brightYellow   ANSI colour 11
    entry #brightBlue   ANSI colour 12
    entry #brightMagenta   ANSI colour 13
    entry #brightCyan   ANSI colour 14
    entry #brightWhite   ANSI colour 15
  entry .Syntax   the roles of a syntax-highlighting palette
    detail
        A self-typed mix-in for `Palette`: one role per token class a highlighter
        distinguishes.
    entry #error   an erroneous token
    entry #number   a numeric literal
    entry #modifier   a modifier keyword
    entry #identifier   an identifier
    entry #term   a term-level keyword
    entry #meta   a meta token: annotations, directives
    entry #string   a string literal
    entry #parenthesis   brackets and parentheses
    entry #symbol   an operator or symbol
    entry #comment   a comment

entry .WebColors   the 141 named colours of CSS, each a `Color in Srgb`
  detail
      The colours a web page may name, from `AliceBlue` to `YellowGreen`, as values of type
      `Color in Srgb`, so each converts, mixes and renders like any other colour, and each is
      `Chromatic`, so it styles terminal text directly. `.colors` lists them all, in
      broadly alphabetical order.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      WebColors.Tomato.to[Hsl].hue.degrees          // 9.1
      WebColors.SteelBlue.to[Srgb].mix(WebColors.White.to[Srgb])
      ```

      The values are the CSS definitions rounded to three decimal places, so `WebColors.Tomato`
      is `Srgb(1, 0.388, 0.278)` and its `Chroma` is `Chroma(255, 98, 70)`, within one of the
      CSS `rgb(255, 99, 71)` in a channel, since the `Chromatic` conversion truncates.
      `Gray` and the other grey names use the American spelling only.
  refer iridescence.Srgb
  refer iridescence.Color
  entry .colors   every named colour, as a list in broadly alphabetical order
