entry .rgb   the `rgb"…"` interpolator: a hexadecimal colour as a `Chroma`, checked as the code compiles
    `rgb"#rrggbb"` or `rgb"rrggbb"` reads six hexadecimal digits, in either case, as the
    red, green and blue channels of a `Chroma`, the whole-number 24-bit colour which
    terminals and images take. The macro parses the literal while the code compiles and
    splices `Chroma(r, g, b)`, so a malformed literal is a compile error: _the color must be
    in the form `rgb"#rrggbb"` or `rgb"rrggbb"` where rr, gg and bb are 2-digit hex values_.
    The interpolator takes no substitutions, _an rgb literal cannot have substitutions_.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    rgb"#abcdef"           // Chroma(171, 205, 239)
    rgb"FFFFFF"            // Chroma(255, 255, 255)
    rgb"#abcdef".color     // Srgb(0.671, 0.804, 0.937), as a Color in Srgb
    rgb"#abcdef".packed    // the same colour as a Pixel[Rgb]
    rgb"#abc"              // does not compile
    ```

    A `Chroma` is `Chromatic`, so the literal styles terminal text as it is
    (`e"${rgb"#ff6347"}(tomato)"`); `color` is the step into the perceptual models,
    `Palette` roles and `Daub` mixing, all of which take a `Color in Srgb`.

entry .color   `chroma.color`: a `Chroma` as a `Color in Srgb`, each channel divided by `255`
    An extension on anticipation's `Chroma`. A `Chroma` is already sRGB, only with
    whole-number channels, so the conversion is one division per channel and loses nothing;
    it is the one step from an `rgb"…"` literal, or any other `Chroma`, into the rest of the
    library.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import colorimetry.daylightColorimetry

    rgb"#ff6347".color.to[Cielab]
    ```

    The way back is the `Color.chromatic` given, which truncates each channel, so a colour
    whose channels are not multiples of `1/255` may come back one lower.

entry .packed   a `Chroma` as a `Pixel[Rgb]`, or an `Rgb32` as a 10/12/10 `Pixel`, with no change to the bits
    Two extensions of one name. `chroma.packed` is the `Pixel[Rgb]` — eight bits per channel
    — of a `Chroma`, and `rgb32.packed` is the `Pixel[(Red[10], Green[12], Blue[10])]` of an
    `Rgb32`. Each type already packs its channels in exactly that layout, so the conversion
    only retypes the bits. `rgb32` goes back from the 10/12/10 pixel.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val pixel: Pixel[Rgb] = rgb"#abcdef".packed
    (pixel.red, pixel.green, pixel.blue)   // (171, 205, 239)
    pixel.chroma                           // Chroma(171, 205, 239)

    Rgb32(600, 3000, 200).packed.rgb32     // Rgb32(600, 3000, 200)
    ```

    The name was `pixel` in earlier releases; it was renamed because the umbrella also
    exports a `pixel` from another library.

entry .rgb32   a 10/12/10 `Pixel` as the `Rgb32` with the same bits
    An extension on `Pixel[(Red[10], Green[12], Blue[10])]`, the inverse of `rgb32.packed`:
    `Rgb32(600, 3000, 200).packed.rgb32` is `Rgb32(600, 3000, 200)`.

entry .Rgb   the pixel layout of 24-bit colour: eight bits each of red, green and blue
    `(Red[8], Green[8], Blue[8])`, the layout of a `Chroma` and of `chroma.packed`. Its
    storage, `anticipation.Channel.Storage[Rgb]`, is `Int`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    Pixel[Rgb](WebColors.Tomato).red   // 255
    ```

entry .Rgba   the pixel layout of 32-bit colour with alpha: eight bits each of red, green, blue and alpha
    `(Red[8], Green[8], Blue[8], Alpha[8])`. `Pixel[Rgba](color)` sets the alpha channel
    fully opaque, since a `Color` has no transparency.

entry .Cmyk8   the pixel layout of 32-bit CMYK: eight bits each of cyan, magenta, yellow and key
    `(Cyan[8], Magenta[8], Yellow[8], Key[8])`. Named `Cmyk8` because `Cmyk` is the
    `Double`-based colour model. `Pixel[Cmyk8](color)` separates the colour into inks as
    `to[Cmyk]` does, and the pixel's `color` is a `Cmyk`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val pixel = Pixel[Cmyk8](Srgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0))
    (pixel.cyan, pixel.magenta, pixel.yellow, pixel.key)   // (0, 255, 255, 0)
    ```

entry .Pixel   one colour packed into an integer in a channel layout: `Pixel[Rgb]`, `Pixel[Rgba]`, `Pixel[Cmyk8]`, …
    `Pixel[layout]` is the opaque type `iridescence.PixelOpaque.Pixel`, exported here: a
    `Long` holding the channels of `layout` — a tuple of anticipation's channel types such
    as `(Red[5], Green[6], Blue[5])`, most significant first — in its low bits. The shift
    and mask of every channel follow from the tuple while the code compiles, so reading a
    channel is a single shift-and-mask with constant operands and no runtime dispatch, and
    a channel the layout does not have is not there to read: `Pixel[Rgb]` has no `alpha`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    type Rgb565 = (Red[5], Green[6], Blue[5])

    val pixel: Pixel[Rgb565] = Pixel[Rgb565](WebColors.Tomato)
    (pixel.red, pixel.green, pixel.blue)   // five, six and five bits
    pixel.chroma                           // rescaled to 24-bit colour, rounding
    pixel.color                            // an Srgb, exact at this depth
    Pixel[Rgb](WebColors.Tomato).alpha     // does not compile
    ```

    `Pixel[layout](color)` packs any `Color in Srgb` into whichever channels the layout
    has: red, green and blue (with any alpha channel fully opaque), cyan, magenta, yellow
    and key, or a single grey channel by Rec. 601 luma. Each proportion is scaled to the
    channel's depth and rounded. `Pixel.make` wraps a raw `Long` already in the layout, and
    `Pixel.value` unwraps one.

    The channel accessors — `red`, `green`, `blue`, `alpha`, `cyan`, `magenta`, `yellow`,
    `key`, `grey`, and `channel["label"]` and `proportion["label"]` for any label — return
    the raw channel value or its fraction of full depth. `color` is the pixel in the natural
    model of its layout (an `Srgb` for RGB and grey layouts, a `Cmyk` for CMYK ones), `srgb`
    is always an `Srgb`, and `chroma` is the 24-bit `Chroma`, rounding as an `Rgb32`'s `chroma`
    does. A pixel is `Chromatic`, so it styles terminal text directly.

    The accessors are `inline`, so they need a concrete layout: code which is itself generic
    in `layout` cannot call them, and should work with the runtime channel descriptions
    instead. This is the representation hallucination's images use for typed pixel access.
    The umbrella also exports a `Channel` of its own (the WebSocket channel), so the layout
    machinery is reached as `anticipation.Channel`.

entry .PixelOpaque   the home of the opaque `Pixel` type, its companion and its channel accessors
  detail
      An object which exists so that `Pixel` can be opaque; the type is exported to the
      `iridescence` package as `Pixel`, whose page has the full account. The extension
      methods declared here apply to any `Pixel[layout]` with no import, through the type's
      implicit scope.
  refer iridescence.Pixel
  entry .Pixel   a colour packed into a `Long` in a channel layout; see `iridescence.Pixel`
    detail
        The opaque type `Pixel[layout <: Tuple] = Long` and its companion.
    entry .underlying   the `Underlying` given which shows a `Pixel` is a `Long` underneath
    entry .chromatic   a `Pixel` is `Chromatic`: its `chroma`, so it styles terminal text directly
    entry .make   `Pixel.make[layout](value)`: a raw `Long` already packed in the layout, as a `Pixel`
        `Pixel.make[(Red[10], Green[12], Blue[10])](1023L << 22 | 2048L << 10 | 512L).red`
        is `1023`. No bits are checked or masked.
    entry .value   the raw `Long` of a `Pixel`
    entry .apply   `Pixel[layout](color)`: a `Color in Srgb` scaled into the layout's channels and packed
        Converts the colour to `Srgb` and packs it into an RGB layout (any alpha channel set
        to full opacity), a CMYK layout (the key the complement of the brightest channel,
        the inks the rest), or a single grey channel (Rec. 601 luma, `0.299·red +
        0.587·green + 0.114·blue`), each proportion scaled to the channel's depth and rounded.
  entry .channel   `pixel.channel["label"]`: the raw value of a labelled channel, for a concrete layout
      Compiles to one shift and one mask with constant operands. A label the layout does not
      contain fails to compile, since the shift cannot be computed.
  entry .red   the red channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .green   the green channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .blue   the blue channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .alpha   the alpha channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .cyan   the cyan channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .magenta   the magenta channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .yellow   the yellow channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .key   the key (black) channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .grey   the grey channel's raw value, in a layout with one
  entry .proportion   `pixel.proportion["label"]`: a channel as a fraction of its full depth, `0`–`1`
      `Pixel.make[(Red[16], Green[16], Blue[16])](65535L << 32).proportion["red"]` is `1.0`.
  entry .color   the pixel in its layout's natural model: `Srgb` for RGB and grey, `Cmyk` for CMYK
      A transparent inline method, so the result's static type is the model itself. Exact at
      any channel depth.
  entry .srgb   the pixel as an `Srgb`, whatever its layout's model
      A CMYK pixel is converted through `Cmyk`; a grey one has equal channels.
  entry .chroma   the pixel rescaled to a 24-bit `Chroma`, rounding to the nearest step
      An RGB layout is rescaled channel by channel, rounding as an `Rgb32`'s `chroma` does; any other
      layout goes through `srgb`. Where the `Color.chromatic` conversion truncates, this
      rounds, so the two may differ by one in a channel.

entry .Rgb12   a colour in twelve bits, four per channel: the `#rgb` of CSS shorthand
    `Rgb12` is the opaque type `iridescence.Rgb12Opaque.Rgb12`, exported here: an `Int`
    holding red, green and blue as four bits each. `Rgb12(red, green, blue)` takes each
    channel in `0`–`15`, keeping only its low four bits, and the extensions `red`, `green`
    and `blue` read them back. `color` gives the `Color in Srgb` (each channel over `15`),
    and `chroma` the 24-bit `Chroma`, each channel times `17`, so `f` becomes `ff`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val violet: Rgb12 = Rgb12(15, 0, 15)
    violet.inspect   // t"#f0f"
    violet.chroma    // Chroma(255, 0, 255)
    violet.color     // Srgb(1, 0, 1)
    ```

    It is `Chromatic`, so it styles terminal text directly, and `Inspectable` as its
    three-digit hexadecimal.

entry .Rgb12Opaque   the home of the opaque `Rgb12` type, its companion and its channel accessors
  detail
      An object which exists so that `Rgb12` can be opaque; the type is exported to the
      `iridescence` package, whose page for `Rgb12` has the full account. The extension
      methods here apply to any `Rgb12` with no import.
  refer iridescence.Rgb12
  entry .Rgb12   a twelve-bit colour in an `Int`; see `iridescence.Rgb12`
    detail
        The opaque type `Rgb12 = Int` and its companion.
    entry .underlying   the `Underlying` given which shows an `Rgb12` is an `Int` underneath
    entry .inspectable   an `Rgb12` inspects as its three-digit hexadecimal, `#f0f`
    entry .chromatic   an `Rgb12` is `Chromatic`, through its `chroma`
    entry .apply   `Rgb12(red, green, blue)`: three four-bit channels, each masked to `0`–`15`
  entry .red   the red channel, `0`–`15`
  entry .green   the green channel, `0`–`15`
  entry .blue   the blue channel, `0`–`15`
  entry .color   the colour as a `Color in Srgb`, each channel divided by `15`
  entry .chroma   the colour as a 24-bit `Chroma`, each channel multiplied by `17`

entry .Rgb32   a colour in thirty-two bits: ten of red, twelve of green, ten of blue
    `Rgb32` is the opaque type `iridescence.Rgb32Opaque.Rgb32`, exported here: an `Int`
    holding red in `0`–`1023`, green in `0`–`4095` and blue in `0`–`1023`, the extra bits
    of green matching the eye's greater sensitivity to it. `Rgb32(red, green, blue)` masks
    each channel to its width, the extensions `red`, `green` and `blue` read them back,
    `color` gives the `Color in Srgb`, and `chroma` the 24-bit `Chroma`, rounding to the
    nearest step.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val colour: Rgb32 = Rgb32(1023, 4095, 0)
    colour.inspect          // t"rgb32(1023, 4095, 0)"
    colour.chroma           // Chroma(255, 255, 0)
    colour.packed.rgb32     // the same Rgb32, through a 10/12/10 Pixel
    ```

    It is `Chromatic`, so it styles terminal text directly, and `Inspectable` as its three
    channels. `packed` retypes it as a `Pixel[(Red[10], Green[12], Blue[10])]`, whose bits
    are the same.

entry .Rgb32Opaque   the home of the opaque `Rgb32` type, its companion and its channel accessors
  detail
      An object which exists so that `Rgb32` can be opaque; the type is exported to the
      `iridescence` package, whose page for `Rgb32` has the full account. The extension
      methods here apply to any `Rgb32` with no import.
  refer iridescence.Rgb32
  entry .Rgb32   a 10/12/10-bit colour in an `Int`; see `iridescence.Rgb32`
    detail
        The opaque type `Rgb32 = Int` and its companion.
    entry .underlying   the `Underlying` given which shows an `Rgb32` is an `Int` underneath
    entry .chromatic   an `Rgb32` is `Chromatic`, through its `chroma`
    entry .inspectable   an `Rgb32` inspects as its three channels, `rgb32(1023, 4095, 0)`
    entry .apply   `Rgb32(red, green, blue)`: channels of ten, twelve and ten bits, each masked to its width
  entry .red   the red channel, `0`–`1023`
  entry .green   the green channel, `0`–`4095`
  entry .blue   the blue channel, `0`–`1023`
  entry .color   the colour as a `Color in Srgb`, each channel divided by its maximum
  entry .chroma   the colour as a 24-bit `Chroma`, each channel rescaled to `0`–`255` and rounded

entry .internal   the macro behind `rgb"…"`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macro has a stable home.
  entry .rgbMacro   parses an `rgb"…"` literal as the code compiles and splices the `Chroma`
      Accepts six hexadecimal digits with or without a leading `#`, and halts compilation
      with a message otherwise, or if the literal has substitutions.
